# Mobile-First Development Rules

## Core Principles

- Always design and develop for mobile devices first, then enhance for larger screens
- Use progressive enhancement: start with mobile layout and add desktop features
- Prioritize touch interactions and mobile usability
- Ensure content is accessible and readable on small screens

## Tailwind CSS Mobile-First Guidelines

### Responsive Design

- Always write mobile styles first (no prefix), then add larger breakpoints
- Use Tailwind's responsive prefixes: `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`, `2xl:`
- Example: `text-sm md:text-base lg:text-lg` (mobile-first approach)

### Layout Patterns

- Use `flex flex-col` for mobile layouts, then `md:flex-row` for desktop
- Implement proper spacing: `p-4 md:p-6 lg:p-8`
- Use `w-full` on mobile, then constrain with `md:max-w-*` on larger screens
- Prefer `space-y-*` over `space-x-*` on mobile for vertical stacking

### Typography

- Start with smaller text sizes: `text-sm` or `text-base` for mobile
- Use `md:text-lg` or `lg:text-xl` for desktop enhancement
- Ensure line height is appropriate: `leading-tight` for mobile, `md:leading-normal` for desktop
- Use `text-center` on mobile, `md:text-left` for desktop when appropriate

### Navigation

- Implement hamburger menu for mobile (`md:hidden`)
- Show full navigation on desktop (`hidden md:flex`)
- Use `fixed` or `sticky` positioning for mobile navigation
- Ensure touch targets are at least 44px (`h-11` or `min-h-[44px]`)

### Forms and Inputs

- Use full width on mobile: `w-full`
- Add proper padding: `p-3` or `p-4`
- Use `text-base` to prevent zoom on iOS
- Implement proper spacing between form elements: `space-y-4`

### Images and Media

- Use `w-full h-auto` for responsive images
- Implement proper aspect ratios: `aspect-square`, `aspect-video`
- Use `object-cover` for consistent image display
- Consider lazy loading for mobile performance

### Touch Interactions

- Ensure minimum touch target size: `min-h-[44px] min-w-[44px]`
- Use `active:` states for touch feedback
- Implement proper hover states that work on touch devices
- Use `cursor-pointer` for interactive elements

### Performance

- Optimize for mobile network speeds
- Use `loading="lazy"` for images below the fold
- Minimize bundle size for mobile users
- Implement proper caching strategies

### Accessibility

- Ensure proper contrast ratios for mobile screens
- Use semantic HTML elements
- Implement proper focus states
- Test with screen readers on mobile devices

## Component Development

- Design components mobile-first
- Use CSS Grid and Flexbox for responsive layouts
- Implement proper component composition
- Test on actual mobile devices, not just browser dev tools

## Testing Checklist

- [ ] Test on actual mobile devices (iOS Safari, Android Chrome)
- [ ] Verify touch interactions work properly
- [ ] Check text readability without zooming
- [ ] Ensure forms are usable on mobile keyboards
- [ ] Test navigation and menu interactions
- [ ] Verify images load and display correctly
- [ ] Check performance on slower mobile connections

## Common Mobile-First Patterns

### Card Layout

```tsx
<div className="w-full p-4 md:p-6 lg:p-8">
  <div className="flex flex-col space-y-4 md:flex-row md:space-y-0 md:space-x-6">
    {/* Content */}
  </div>
</div>
```

### Responsive Grid

```tsx
<div className="grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
  {/* Grid items */}
</div>
```

### Mobile Navigation

```tsx
<nav className="md:hidden">
  {/* Mobile menu */}
</nav>
<nav className="hidden md:flex">
  {/* Desktop menu */}
</nav>
```

### Responsive Typography

```tsx
<h1 className="text-2xl font-bold md:text-3xl lg:text-4xl">Title</h1>
```

Remember: Mobile-first means starting with the smallest screen and working up, not just making desktop designs smaller.
